It's random on purpose. The runtime picks a random starting bucket for each iteration, so the same map prints in different orders on different runs. Early Go had a deterministic order; people wrote code that depended on it; the Go team made it explicitly randomized so that dependency could never form silently. Now the spec says order is unspecified — treat every iteration order as an accident.

When order matters, the pattern is fixed and small:

```go
keys := make([]string, 0, len(m))
for k := range m {
    keys = append(keys, k)
}
sort.Strings(keys)
for _, k := range keys {
    fmt.Println(k, m[k])
}
```

The reason this matters in interviews and code review: code that 'worked' in testing with 3 keys can break in production with 300, because iteration order differences expose hidden assumptions — first-key-wins logic, dedup-by-iteration, pretty-printing diffs. If two runs of a test on the same map can disagree, the test is testing an accident.

The two-value lookup answers presence and value separately:

```go
v, ok := m[key]
```

ok is a bool: true means the key exists (and v holds its value — even if that value is the zero value), false means it doesn't (and v is the type's zero).

The reason it exists: the one-value lookup collapses 'missing' and 'stored zero' into the same result. m["admin"] is false whether the key is absent or present-with-false — and those are usually very different situations. The comma-ok form splits them back apart.

```go
// ambiguous — rejects absent AND opted-out identically
if !settings["notifications"] { ... }

// precise
if enabled, ok := settings["notifications"]; ok {
    use(enabled)
} else {
    applyDefault()  // key never set
}
```

Use it whenever a stored zero value is legitimate data — optional flags, counters at zero, empty strings that mean something. For accumulate patterns (m[w]++) the one-value form is correct because 'default to zero' is exactly what you want.

Why is delete total while write panics on nil? The asymmetry has the same shape as the nil-map read rule: operations that need to 'find a place for something' require the structure to exist; operations that can honestly answer 'nothing there' don't.

Delete is a lookup plus an unlink: probe for the key; if absent, there's nothing to unlink — done. No structure needed to express 'no effect.' Write, by contrast, needs a bucket to put the entry into — hence the panic on nil.

The iterate-while-deleting rule comes from the iterator's contract: entries deleted before the iterator reaches them may or may not be visited (unspecified); entries that exist and aren't deleted will be. That's enough to write cleanup loops safely:

```go
for k, v := range cache {
    if v.expired() {
        delete(cache, k)
    }
}
```

The pattern works because deletion can't invalidate the iteration — the iterator holds its position in the bucket structure, and delete unlinks entries without moving where subsequent iteration resumes. Insert during iteration is the unspecified case to avoid.
